ng, and you need to use Name and Source attributes. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example: </br> <br> \u2022\t<i><strong>If</strong> you <strong>need to</strong> add extra settings to a webpage, you <strong>can use </strong>directives in ASP.NET</br> \u2022\tYou <strong>can use</strong> directives in ASP.NET <strong>if </strong>you <strong>need to</strong> add extra settings to a webpage. </i></br> "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Notice that when the conditional clause goes at the beginning, you must add a comma before the result clause. </br></br>  These are the structures when using the <b>zero conditional</b> : </br></br> <center> <strong>If/ When</strong> Clause in Simple Present (Condition) + Simple Present  (Result)</br>  </center></br></br> <center>Simple Present (Result) + <strong>if/when</strong> Clause in Simple Present (Condition) </br> </center> </br></br>  The word <b>if</b> can be replaced by <strong>when</strong> without changing the condition meaning: </br></br>  \u2022\t<i><strong>If</strong> he wants to describe how the controls are processed, he has to program directives. </br> \u2022\t<strong>When </strong>he wants to describe how the controls are processed, he has to program directives. </i>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Gerunds After Prepositions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "Every time that there is a verb after a preposition, the verb has to take the <strong>gerund or verb +ING</strong> form. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "A <strong>gerund</strong> represents a noun-phrase in a sentence and it can always be replaced by an actual noun.</br></br>  "}, {"type": "example", "content": "For instance, you use them for registering a custom control or for specifying page language. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "It is necessary to manage the user controls in the .Net framework before programming a setting with this directive."}, {"type": "text", "content": "These are some examples on how using gerunds or names after prepositions: </br></br>  <b>For</b></br> <i> \u2022\tWe use directives <strong>for adding</strong> extra settings in web pages or applications. </br> \u2022\tWe use directives <strong>for the addition</strong> of extra settings in web pages or applications.</i> </br></br>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<b>  After</b></br>  \u2022<i>\t<strong>After setting </strong>directives, you can register custom controls. </br> \u2022\t<strong>After the setting </strong>of directives, you can register custom controls.</i> </br></br>  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "<b> Before</b> </br>  \u2022<i>\t<strong>Before taking</strong> the test on ASP.NET directives, you need to review all types. </br> \u2022\t<strong>Before the examination</strong> on ASP.NET directives, you need to review all types. </i>"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "directive "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "an order or direction  "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: We can say that ASP.NET directives are commands that programmers use to add optional settings to a website."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob11aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "custom "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "made with a particular purpose"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(adjective)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You use them for registering a custom control or for specifying page language."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob11aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "attribute "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a particular characteristic to set the properties of objects, elements or files in computing "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: For example, there is a type called Application Directive. It is used to specify application specific-attributes such as Inherits, Description and Language."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob11aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "parse "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to analyze data by putting it into smaller chunks to be easily manipulated "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: When you use it, you can connect an assembly to a page or application when parsing."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob11aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "point out "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to indicate something specifically"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(phrasal verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: If you set an Implement Directive, you point out that your page needs to use a specific interface. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob11aud05"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "namespace "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a group of symbols to organize files, variables or resources "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: In the case of the Import one, it can introduce a namespace into a page or application."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob11aud06"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I look for more information about ASP.NET directives. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I identify key information about each type of directive and take notes. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I create a slider tutorial to explain each type of directive, its use and attributes.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I prepare an oral presentation to present my slider tutorial.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I share my slider tutorial with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "Look for more information about ASP.NET directives. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Identify key information about each type of directive and take notes. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Create a slider tutorial to explain each type of directive, its use and attributes.  "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Prepare an oral presentation to present your slider tutorial. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Share your slider tutorial with the class. "}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "type": "welcome", "_data": [], "contents": [{}], "id": 0}, {"template_override": 1, "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"time": "100", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob11re2img01.jpg", "id": 0, "text": "<strong>David</strong>: What's the matter, Laura? You look stressed. </br>\n<strong>Laura</strong>: I'm really nervous. I'm applying for a position in a software development company, and I have an admission test tomorrow. </br>\n<strong>David</strong>: I see. Is there anything I can help you with? </br>\n<strong>Laura</strong>: Yes. I need to review some aspects about ASP.NET directives. Could you give me a hand with that? </br>\n<strong>David</strong>: Sure. Actually, I had to write a paper about that for my computer programming class. </br>\n<strong>Laura</strong>: Perfect! How would you define the ASP.NET directives? </br>\n<strong>David</strong>: We can say that ASP.NET directives are commands that programmers use to add optional settings to a website; for instance, you use them for registering a custom control or for specifying page language. </br>"}, {"time": "49000", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob11re2img02.jpg", "id": 1, "text": "<strong>Laura</strong>: OK. So, if I understand you correctly, you are saying that these settings can define the way in which web forms or user controls are run by the .Net framework. Right? </br>\n<strong>David</strong>: Exactly.  That is the reason why there are different kinds of directives. For example, there is a type called Application Directive. It is used to specify application specific-attributes such as Inherits, Description and Language. </br>\n<strong>Laura</strong>: Right. I remember. There is also an Assembly Directive.  When you use it, you can connect an assembly to a page or application when parsing, and you need to use Name and Source attributes. </br>"}, {"time": "93500", "image": "enpe7n6le01ob11re2img03.jpg", "id": 2, "text": "<strong>David</strong>: Well-done! Let's continue with the Control Directive. It is necessary to manage the user controls in the .Net framework before programming a setting with this directive. There are plenty of attributes for the Control directive, for example, ClassName, Debug, Explicit or Strict. </br>\n<strong>Laura</strong>: Thanks. I guess some types are missing. </br>\n<strong>David</strong>: Yes, we have also Implements, Import, and Master Directives. If you set the Implement Directive, you point out that your page needs to use a specific interface. In the case of the Import one, it can introduce a namespace into a page or application. </br>\n<strong>Laura</strong>: I see. I guess the Master Directive is the one we use for defining a page as the master one. </br>\n<strong>David</strong>: You're right. I think you are going to do pretty well at the test; you remember things easily."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n6le01ob11re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le01ob11re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "type": "audio", "_data": [], "id": 1, "title": ""}], "exercises": [{"stimuli_ids": [0], "template_override": 1, "questions": [], "type": "WLC", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can say what directives are in ASP.NET.   "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can list some of the directives in ASP.NET.  "}, {"type": "Learning Outcomes", "content": "I can talk about characteristics of some directives in ASP.NET."}, {"type": "Free Header Item", "content": "How do you provide instructions when creating a webpage?"}, {"type": "Free 